      "image": "videos/01-brain-decides-first/2.jpg",
      "seconds": 36.21,
      "text": "In 2008, neuroscientists in Berlin placed volunteers inside a brain scanner and gave them one simple choice. Left button... or right. Then they watched. Activity in the frontal cortex predicted the choice up to seven seconds before people felt they had decided. Not perfectly, but far better than chance. Decades earlier, Benjamin Libet had caught the same signal, rising in the brain before the conscious urge to move.",
